  "name": "Kokyoku (Itchū, Katō, Miyazono and Ogie-bushi)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Kokyoku, the 'old songs', groups four early Japanese shamisen narrative and lyric styles kept in small lineages: Itchū-bushi, founded in Kyoto at the turn of the eighteenth century by Miyako Itchū; Katō-bushi, from early eighteenth-century Edo; Miyazono-bushi, a Kyoto jōruri of love-suicide pieces; and Ogie-bushi, a parlour style derived from nagauta in late eighteenth-century Edo. The four are designated together as an Important Intangible Cultural Property.",
  "recipe": "classic East Asian early-1950s kokyoku, art Music, east Asian branch. narrative. cat skin ivory shamisen. tatami parlor japanese. Neumann U47, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips plate reverb.",
